export default "# CLOUD NETWORKING: INTERMEDIATE PATTERNS AND COMPOSITION\n\n# PART 1 OF 4: NETWORK COMPOSITION AND ARCHITECTURE PATTERNS\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nIn previous lessons, we studied:\n\n- Virtual Private Clouds (VPCs).\n- Subnets and CIDR notation.\n- Routing tables.\n- Internet gateways.\n- Security groups.\n- Firewalls.\n- Inbound and outbound traffic.\n- Stateful and stateless network security.\n\nThese components are important, but production systems rarely use them independently.\n\nA real cloud environment combines networking components into an architecture.\n\nConsider a production marketplace:\n\n- Customers access a frontend.\n- The frontend communicates with an API.\n- The API communicates with databases.\n- Background workers process orders.\n- Administrators connect to private servers.\n- Monitoring systems collect application and network information.\n\nAll these components require controlled communication.\n\nThis is called **network composition**.\n\nNetwork composition is the process of combining networking services, infrastructure, routing, security controls, and communication mechanisms to create a complete network architecture.\n\n## 2. Understanding Network Architecture Patterns\n\nA network architecture pattern is a reusable design for connecting infrastructure components.\n\nDifferent patterns solve different problems.\n\n| Pattern | Main purpose |\n|---|---|\n| Public-private subnet pattern | Separate internet-facing and internal resources |\n| Three-tier network pattern | Separate frontend, application, and database layers |\n| Hub-and-spoke pattern | Connect multiple networks through a central network |\n| Multi-zone pattern | Improve availability across zones |\n| Hybrid networking pattern | Connect cloud infrastructure to on-premises systems |\n| Private service connectivity | Connect services without exposing them publicly |\n| Centralized egress pattern | Control outbound internet traffic |\n\nLet's examine each.\n\n## 3. Public and Private Network Composition\n\nA common cloud architecture separates resources into public and private subnets.\n\n### Public subnet\n\nA public subnet is a subnet whose routing configuration provides a path to an internet gateway.\n\nResources placed there may be internet-accessible if their addressing, security policies, and service configuration permit it.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Public load balancers.\n- Public web servers when necessary.\n- Bastion hosts, when this access model is selected.\n\n### Private subnet\n\nA private subnet does not provide direct internet reachability through a public IP and an internet gateway.\n\nIt is commonly used for internal resources.\n\nExamples:\n\n- Backend application servers.\n- Databases.\n- Background workers.\n- Internal services.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```text\n                    INTERNET\n                        |\n                        v\n                 INTERNET GATEWAY\n                        |\n                        v\n                 PUBLIC SUBNET\n                        |\n                 PUBLIC LOAD BALANCER\n                        |\n                        v\n                 PRIVATE SUBNET\n                        |\n                  BACKEND API\n                        |\n                        v\n                  PRIVATE SUBNET\n                        |\n                     DATABASE\n```\n\nThe frontend entry point receives public traffic.\n\nThe backend and database are protected inside private network segments.\n\n**Important:** A subnet is not automatically secure because it is called private. Security groups, routes, network ACLs, service configuration, and identity controls must all be considered.\n\n## 4. Three-Tier Network Architecture\n\nThree-tier architecture separates an application into three logical layers.\n\n| Tier | Responsibility | Example |\n|---|---|---|\n| Presentation | User interface | Frontend |\n| Application | Business logic | Backend API |\n| Data | Persistent information | Database |\n\n### Example\n\nImagine a marketplace application.\n\n**Presentation tier**\n\nCustomers browse products, create accounts, and submit orders.\n\n**Application tier**\n\nThe backend processes:\n- Authentication.\n- Product management.\n- Payments.\n- Orders.\n- Notifications.\n\n**Data tier**\n\nThe database stores:\n- Customer records.\n- Product information.\n- Orders.\n- Payment references.\n\n### Network composition\n\n```text\n                 PUBLIC INTERNET\n                        |\n                        v\n                 LOAD BALANCER\n                        |\n                        v\n                 PRESENTATION\n                  FRONTEND\n                        |\n                        v\n                 APPLICATION TIER\n                    BACKEND\n                        |\n                        v\n                     DATA TIER\n                     DATABASE\n```\n\nEach tier should have its own access policy.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Source | Destination | Port | Permission |\n|---|---|---:|---|\n| Internet | Load balancer | 443 | Allow |\n| Load balancer | Backend | 3000 | Allow |\n| Backend | Database | 27017 | Allow |\n| Internet | Database | 27017 | Deny |\n\nThis is called network segmentation.\n\n## 5. Network Segmentation\n\nNetwork segmentation means dividing a network into smaller sections with controlled communication between them.\n\nThe objective is to prevent unnecessary access between systems.\n\nImagine that a company has:\n\n- Production servers.\n- Development servers.\n- Database servers.\n- Monitoring servers.\n- Administrative systems.\n\nPutting everything into one unrestricted network creates unnecessary risk.\n\nInstead, separate the systems.\n\n### Example network segments\n\n| Segment | CIDR | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Public | `10.0.1.0/24` | Public entry services |\n| Application | `10.0.2.0/24` | Backend services |\n| Database | `10.0.3.0/24` | Database servers |\n| Management | `10.0.4.0/24` | Administrative access |\n| Monitoring | `10.0.5.0/24` | Monitoring infrastructure |\n\nThese are example private IPv4 ranges.\n\n### Why segmentation matters\n\nSuppose an attacker compromises the frontend.\n\nWithout segmentation, the attacker might attempt to access every other server.\n\nWith segmentation, the attacker may encounter restrictions preventing direct database or management access.\n\nSegmentation helps limit lateral movement.\n\n**Lateral movement** is when an attacker moves from one compromised system to other systems inside an environment.\n\n## 6. Hub-and-Spoke Architecture\n\nHub-and-spoke is a networking pattern where several networks connect through a central network.\n\nThe central network is called the hub.\n\nThe connected networks are called spokes.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```text\n                 DEVELOPMENT VPC\n                        |\n                        |\n                        v\nPRODUCTION VPC ---- CENTRAL HUB ---- TESTING VPC\n                        |\n                        |\n                        v\n                 MANAGEMENT VPC\n```\n\nThe hub provides shared networking services.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- Centralized routing.\n- Network inspection.\n- Shared DNS.\n- Centralized egress.\n- Connectivity to on-premises systems.\n\n### Why use hub-and-spoke?\n\nImagine a company has five independent cloud environments.\n\nConnecting every network directly to every other network can become difficult to maintain.\n\nA hub creates a central communication point.\n\nAdvantages:\n\n- Centralized network management.\n- Shared security controls.\n- Easier network inspection.\n- Reduced complexity for many connected networks.\n- Centralized access to shared services.\n\nDisadvantages:\n\n- The hub can become a bottleneck.\n- Misconfiguration can affect many connected networks.\n- Additional routing and operational complexity.\n- The hub requires high availability planning.\n\n### Cloud implementations\n\nExamples include:\n\n- AWS Transit Gateway.\n- Azure Virtual WAN and hub virtual networks.\n- Google Cloud Network Connectivity Center.\n\nThese services have different capabilities and should not be treated as identical products.\n\n## 7. Spoke-to-Spoke Communication\n\nConsider three networks:\n\n```text\n       PRODUCTION\n            |\n            |\n            v\n       CENTRAL HUB\n         /     \\\n        /       \\\n       v         v\n DEVELOPMENT   TESTING\n```\n\nDevelopment may need to communicate with testing.\n\nThere are two possible approaches:\n\n1. Allow communication through the hub.\n2. Prevent communication between spokes unless explicitly required.\n\nFor security, the second approach is often a safer default.\n\nOnly permit the connections required by the business.\n\nFor example, the development environment should not automatically have unrestricted access to the production database.\n\n## 8. Multi-Availability-Zone Networking\n\nCloud providers operate infrastructure across geographical regions and availability zones.\n\nAn availability zone is an isolated infrastructure location within a cloud region.\n\nA production application should avoid depending entirely on one availability zone when higher availability is required.\n\n### Single-zone design\n\n```text\n             CLOUD REGION\n                  |\n                  v\n             ZONE A\n                  |\n             APPLICATION\n                  |\n              DATABASE\n```\n\nIf Zone A experiences an outage, the application may become unavailable.\n\n### Multi-zone design\n\n```text\n                  CLOUD REGION\n                       |\n                 LOAD BALANCER\n                    /      \\\n                   /        \\\n                  v          v\n               ZONE A      ZONE B\n                  |           |\n                  v           v\n              APP SERVER  APP SERVER\n                  \\           /\n                   \\         /\n                    v       v\n                 DATABASE SERVICE\n```\n\nThis design distributes application capacity across zones.\n\nA production database also needs an appropriate high-availability configuration, such as a managed multi-zone deployment or a properly designed replication and failover architecture.\n\nSimply placing two application servers in separate zones does not guarantee that the complete application is highly available.\n\n## 9. Centralized Egress Architecture\n\nEgress means traffic leaving a network or protected environment.\n\nA centralized egress architecture routes outbound internet traffic through a controlled location.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\n             APPLICATION VPC\n                    |\n                    v\n              CENTRAL HUB\n                    |\n                    v\n             NAT / FIREWALL\n                    |\n                    v\n                 INTERNET\n```\n\nBenefits include:\n\n- Centralized outbound security policies.\n- Traffic monitoring.\n- Logging.\n- Destination restrictions.\n- Centralized internet access management.\n\nThis can be useful in organizations with many application networks.\n\nHowever, centralized egress must be designed to avoid bottlenecks and single points of failure.\n\n## 10. Network Address Translation\n\nNetwork Address Translation (NAT) changes IP address information as traffic passes through a network device.\n\nOne common cloud use case is allowing private servers to access the internet without assigning them public IP addresses.\n\n### Example\n\nA private application server has:\n\n```text\nPrivate IP: 10.0.2.10\n```\n\nIt needs to download software updates.\n\nInstead of assigning it a public IP, its outbound traffic passes through a NAT gateway.\n\n```text\nPRIVATE SERVER\n10.0.2.10\n     |\n     v\nNAT GATEWAY\n     |\n     v\nINTERNET\n```\n\nThe NAT gateway translates the private source address to an address it can use for external communication.\n\nExternal systems see the NAT gateway's public address rather than the private server's address.\n\n### Important distinction\n\nNAT does not replace a firewall.\n\nA NAT gateway is not a complete security policy system.\n\nYou still need appropriate security groups, firewall rules, routing, and application security.\n\n## 11. Network Composition in a Marketplace\n\nLet's combine the concepts.\n\nYour marketplace has:\n\n- Public load balancer.\n- Two backend application servers.\n- MongoDB database.\n- Background worker.\n- Monitoring service.\n- Administrative access.\n\nA possible architecture:\n\n```text\n                      INTERNET\n                          |\n                          v\n                   INTERNET GATEWAY\n                          |\n                          v\n                  PUBLIC LOAD BALANCER\n                          |\n             +------------+------------+\n             |                         |\n             v                         v\n        PRIVATE APP A             PRIVATE APP B\n          ZONE A                     ZONE B\n             |                         |\n             +------------+------------+\n                          |\n                          v\n                   PRIVATE DATABASE\n                          |\n                          v\n                       MONGODB\n\n                BACKGROUND WORKER\n                       |\n                       v\n                   PRIVATE QUEUE\n\n              ADMINISTRATIVE NETWORK\n                       |\n                       v\n                CONTROLLED ACCESS\n```\n\nThe architecture combines:\n\n- Public-private subnet separation.\n- Network segmentation.\n- Multi-zone application deployment.\n- Private database access.\n- Controlled administration.\n- Internal service communication.\n\n## Part 1 Summary\n\nYou should now understand:\n\n- Network composition.\n- Public and private network patterns.\n- Three-tier architecture.\n- Network segmentation.\n- Hub-and-spoke networking.\n- Multi-zone architecture.\n- Centralized egress.\n- NAT.\n- Combining networking components into a production architecture.\n\n**Progress: Part 1 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 2 OF 4: CONNECTIVITY, DNS, LOAD BALANCING AND SERVICE COMMUNICATION\n\n## 1. Introduction to Private Connectivity\n\nApplications frequently need to communicate without exposing services publicly.\n\nFor example:\n\n- A backend connects to a database.\n- A monitoring service connects to internal servers.\n- Two VPCs communicate.\n- A company connects its office network to cloud infrastructure.\n\nThese connections are called private connectivity patterns.\n\nPrivate connectivity allows systems to communicate through controlled network paths.\n\n## 2. VPC Peering\n\nVPC peering connects two Virtual Private Clouds so that resources can communicate using private IP addresses.\n\nImagine two VPCs:\n\n```text\nVPC A: 10.0.0.0/16\n\nVPC B: 10.1.0.0/16\n```\n\nA peering connection is established between them.\n\n```text\n       VPC A\n    10.0.0.0/16\n          |\n          |\n      PEERING\n      CONNECTION\n          |\n          |\n       VPC B\n    10.1.0.0/16\n```\n\nThe networks can communicate when the following are correctly configured:\n\n- Peering connection.\n- Route tables.\n- Security groups.\n- Network ACLs or equivalent controls.\n- DNS settings, if private DNS resolution is required.\n\n### Example\n\nAn application in VPC A needs to access an internal reporting service in VPC B.\n\nA route in VPC A might point:\n\n```text\nDestination: 10.1.0.0/16\nTarget: VPC Peering Connection\n```\n\nA corresponding return route is needed in VPC B.\n\n### Important limitation: Non-transitive peering\n\nSuppose:\n\n```text\nVPC A ---- VPC B ---- VPC C\n```\n\nVPC A cannot automatically use VPC B as a router to reach VPC C through ordinary VPC peering.\n\nThis is known as the non-transitive nature of VPC peering.\n\nFor more complex network connectivity, organizations may use a transit gateway or another supported routing architecture.\n\n## 3. VPN Networking\n\nVPN stands for Virtual Private Network.\n\nA VPN creates an encrypted communication tunnel between networks or devices over an underlying network.\n\nIt is frequently used for:\n\n- Remote administrator access.\n- Connecting offices to cloud environments.\n- Connecting separate cloud networks.\n- Hybrid cloud networking.\n\n### Remote-access VPN\n\nAn administrator connects to a VPN service before accessing private cloud resources.\n\n```text\nADMINISTRATOR\n      |\n      v\n   INTERNET\n      |\n      v\n   VPN GATEWAY\n      |\n      v\n PRIVATE CLOUD\n      |\n      v\n INTERNAL SERVERS\n```\n\nThe administrator can access approved private resources without exposing those services directly to the internet.\n\n### Site-to-site VPN\n\nA site-to-site VPN connects two networks.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nCOMPANY OFFICE\n      |\n      v\nVPN DEVICE\n      |\n      v\nENCRYPTED TUNNEL\n      |\n      v\nCLOUD VPN GATEWAY\n      |\n      v\nCLOUD VPC\n```\n\nThis allows office systems to communicate with cloud resources.\n\n### VPN technologies\n\nCommon technologies include:\n\n- IPsec.\n- WireGuard.\n- OpenVPN.\n\nCloud providers also offer managed VPN services.\n\nA VPN provides protected network transport, but it does not replace application authentication or authorization.\n\n## 4. Bastion Hosts\n\nA bastion host is a specially secured server used as a controlled entry point into private infrastructure.\n\nIt is sometimes called a jump server.\n\nInstead of exposing every private server to the internet, administrators connect through the bastion host.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```text\n             ADMINISTRATOR\n                    |\n                    v\n                 INTERNET\n                    |\n                    v\n               BASTION HOST\n                    |\n                    v\n             PRIVATE NETWORK\n                    |\n             +------+------+\n             |             |\n             v             v\n          APP SERVER   DATABASE SERVER\n```\n\nThe bastion host should have:\n\n- Restricted administrator access.\n- Strong authentication.\n- Logging.\n- Regular patching.\n- Minimal installed software.\n- Carefully restricted outbound and internal access.\n\n### SSH example\n\nFirst connect to the bastion:\n\n```bash\nssh admin@203.0.113.20\n```\n\nThen connect to the private application server:\n\n```bash\nssh admin@10.0.2.10\n```\n\nThe second connection happens from the bastion host.\n\nA more convenient approach is SSH ProxyJump:\n\n```bash\nssh -J admin@203.0.113.20 admin@10.0.2.10\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `ssh` starts the SSH client.\n- `-J` specifies a jump host.\n- `admin@203.0.113.20` identifies the bastion.\n- `admin@10.0.2.10` identifies the final private destination.\n\nSSH ProxyJump establishes the connection through the bastion without requiring you to open a separate interactive shell first.\n\n**Security note:** Managed access solutions such as AWS Systems Manager Session Manager or Azure Bastion may be preferable to operating your own public jump server, depending on the environment.\n\n## 5. DNS in Cloud Networking\n\nDNS stands for Domain Name System.\n\nIt translates domain names into information used to locate network services.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\napi.marketplace.example\n```\n\nMay resolve to:\n\n```text\n203.0.113.50\n```\n\nDNS allows applications to use meaningful names instead of manually maintaining IP addresses.\n\n### Basic DNS resolution\n\n```text\nUSER\n |\n v\napi.marketplace.example\n |\n v\nDNS RESOLVER\n |\n v\nIP ADDRESS\n |\n v\nAPI SERVER\n```\n\n### Common DNS record types\n\n| Record | Purpose |\n|---|---|\n| A | Maps a hostname to an IPv4 address |\n| AAAA | Maps a hostname to an IPv6 address |\n| CNAME | Maps one hostname to another hostname |\n| MX | Identifies mail servers |\n| TXT | Stores text information, often used for verification |\n| NS | Identifies authoritative name servers |\n| PTR | Supports reverse DNS lookups |\n| SRV | Describes services, including ports and targets |\n\n### Example DNS records\n\n```text\napi.example.com      A       203.0.113.50\nwww.example.com      CNAME   example.com\nmail.example.com     A       203.0.113.60\n```\n\nThese are illustrative records.\n\n### Check DNS resolution\n\n```bash\nnslookup api.example.com\n```\n\nThis queries DNS for the domain.\n\nAnother option:\n\n```bash\ndig api.example.com\n```\n\n`dig` provides detailed DNS query and response information.\n\n### Private DNS\n\nCloud environments can use private DNS zones.\n\nA private DNS zone allows internal resources to resolve names that are not publicly available.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndatabase.internal\n```\n\nResolves to:\n\n```text\n10.0.3.10\n```\n\nThis is useful because applications can connect using stable service names rather than hard-coded IP addresses.\n\nIf the database address changes, the DNS record can be updated without changing every application configuration.\n\n## 6. Load Balancing\n\nA load balancer distributes incoming network traffic across multiple backend servers.\n\nImagine an application receiving thousands of requests.\n\nSending every request to one server could overload that server.\n\nA load balancer distributes requests across available instances.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```text\n               USERS\n                 |\n                 v\n            LOAD BALANCER\n              /       \\\n             /         \\\n            v           v\n        SERVER A     SERVER B\n            \\           /\n             \\         /\n              v       v\n              DATABASE\n```\n\n### Why use a load balancer?\n\n- Distribute traffic.\n- Improve availability.\n- Support horizontal scaling.\n- Perform health checks.\n- Provide a stable entry point.\n- Support TLS termination in appropriate configurations.\n\n### Health checks\n\nA health check determines whether a backend server is functioning sufficiently to receive traffic.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nGET /health\n```\n\nThe application might return:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"healthy\"\n}\n```\n\nThe load balancer can use this endpoint to determine whether the server should receive requests.\n\nA production health endpoint should be designed carefully. A simple liveness check and a deeper readiness check may serve different purposes.\n\n### Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing\n\n**Layer 4 load balancing**\n\nOperates at the transport layer.\n\nIt can distribute TCP or UDP connections.\n\n**Layer 7 load balancing**\n\nOperates at the application layer.\n\nIt can make routing decisions using information such as:\n\n- HTTP hostnames.\n- URL paths.\n- HTTP headers.\n- Application-level request properties.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\napi.example.com/users\n          |\n          v\n       LOAD BALANCER\n          |\n          v\n      USER SERVICE\n```\n\nAnother request:\n\n```text\napi.example.com/products\n          |\n          v\n       LOAD BALANCER\n          |\n          v\n     PRODUCT SERVICE\n```\n\n## 7. Reverse Proxies\n\nA reverse proxy receives requests from clients and forwards them to backend services.\n\nCommon reverse proxy software includes:\n\n- Nginx.\n- HAProxy.\n- Traefik.\n- Envoy.\n\n### Architecture\n\n```text\nCLIENT\n  |\n  v\nNGINX REVERSE PROXY\n  |\n  +----------+\n  |          |\n  v          v\nAPI A      API B\n```\n\nA reverse proxy can provide:\n\n- Request routing.\n- TLS termination.\n- Compression.\n- Connection management.\n- Caching.\n- Load balancing.\n- Access logging.\n\n### Nginx example\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name api.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://10.0.2.10:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n    }\n}\n```\n\nLine-by-line explanation:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n```\n\nStarts a server configuration block.\n\n```nginx\nlisten 80;\n```\n\nInstructs Nginx to listen on port 80.\n\n```nginx\nserver_name api.example.com;\n```\n\nSpecifies the hostname handled by this server block.\n\n```nginx\nlocation / {\n```\n\nMatches requests under the root URL path.\n\n```nginx\nproxy_pass http://10.0.2.10:3000;\n```\n\nForwards matching requests to the backend API.\n\n```nginx\nproxy_set_header Host $host;\n```\n\nPasses the original host header to the backend.\n\n```nginx\nproxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n```\n\nPasses the client's connection IP as an additional header.\n\nThe application must be configured to trust forwarded headers only from known proxies. Otherwise, clients may spoof those headers.\n\n**Production note:** This example listens on HTTP port 80. A real public deployment should normally use an appropriately configured HTTPS listener and certificate.\n\n## 8. Service-to-Service Communication\n\nModern applications frequently contain multiple services.\n\nFor example:\n\n- User service.\n- Product service.\n- Payment service.\n- Notification service.\n- Order service.\n\nThese services communicate over the network.\n\n### Example architecture\n\n```text\n                 FRONTEND\n                     |\n                     v\n                 API GATEWAY\n                     |\n        +------------+------------+\n        |            |            |\n        v            v            v\n      USERS       PRODUCTS      ORDERS\n      SERVICE      SERVICE      SERVICE\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                            PAYMENT SERVICE\n                                  |\n                                  v\n                            NOTIFICATION\n```\n\nEach service should communicate only with the services it requires.\n\nFor example:\n\n| Source | Destination | Purpose |\n|---|---|---|\n| Frontend | API Gateway | User requests |\n| API Gateway | User Service | Authentication/profile |\n| API Gateway | Product Service | Product information |\n| Order Service | Payment Service | Payment processing |\n| Order Service | Notification Service | Order notifications |\n\nThis is an example of network-level service composition.\n\nApplication-level authentication must still verify that a service is authorized to perform a requested operation.\n\n## 9. Private Service Connectivity\n\nSometimes an application needs to access a cloud-managed service without routing traffic through the public internet.\n\nCloud providers offer private connectivity mechanisms.\n\nExamples include:\n\n- AWS PrivateLink.\n- Azure Private Link.\n- Google Cloud Private Service Connect.\n\nThese technologies provide private connectivity to supported services.\n\n### Example\n\nYour application runs in a private VPC and needs to connect to a managed payment-related internal service.\n\nInstead of exposing the service publicly, a supported private endpoint can provide a private network path.\n\n```text\nPRIVATE APPLICATION\n        |\n        v\nPRIVATE ENDPOINT\n        |\n        v\nPRIVATE SERVICE\n```\n\nThe exact connectivity model and supported services depend on the cloud provider.\n\nPrivate connectivity does not automatically authorize requests. Authentication, authorization, DNS, and service policies remain important.\n\n## 10. Network Observability\n\nNetwork observability is the ability to understand what is happening across network infrastructure.\n\nIt helps engineers identify:\n\n- Connection failures.\n- High latency.\n- Packet loss.\n- DNS failures.\n- Unusual traffic.\n- Routing problems.\n- Unexpected traffic patterns.\n\n### Important monitoring areas\n\n| Area | What it measures |\n|---|---|\n| Latency | Time taken for communication |\n| Packet loss | Packets that fail to reach their destination |\n| Throughput | Amount of data transferred |\n| Connection count | Active or attempted connections |\n| DNS response time | Time required to resolve names |\n| Firewall logs | Allowed and blocked traffic |\n| Network flow logs | Traffic metadata between endpoints |\n\n### Useful Linux commands\n\nCheck routes:\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nCheck listening ports:\n\n```bash\nsudo ss -tulnp\n```\n\nCheck DNS:\n\n```bash\ndig api.example.com\n```\n\nTest reachability:\n\n```bash\nping 10.0.2.10\n```\n\nTest a TCP port:\n\n```bash\nnc -vz 10.0.2.10 3000\n```\n\nTrace the network path:\n\n```bash\ntraceroute 10.0.2.10\n```\n\nNote that ICMP may be blocked, so an unsuccessful ping does not necessarily mean a service is unreachable. Some traceroute methods may also be blocked by firewalls.\n\n## Part 2 Summary\n\nYou have learned:\n\n- VPC peering.\n- VPN connectivity.\n- Bastion hosts.\n- DNS and private DNS.\n- Load balancing.\n- Reverse proxies.\n- Service-to-service communication.\n- Private service connectivity.\n- Network observability.\n\n**Progress: Part 2 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 3 OF 4: PRACTICAL NETWORK COMPOSITION AND AUTOMATION\n\n## 1. Practical Scenario\n\nYou are deploying a marketplace application.\n\nYour infrastructure contains:\n\n| Component | Private IP |\n|---|---|\n| Frontend server | `10.0.1.10` |\n| Backend API | `10.0.2.10` |\n| MongoDB | `10.0.3.10` |\n| Worker | `10.0.4.10` |\n| Monitoring server | `10.0.5.10` |\n\nYou must configure communication between these components.\n\nThe target design:\n\n```text\n                   INTERNET\n                       |\n                       v\n                 LOAD BALANCER\n                       |\n                       v\n                 FRONTEND TIER\n                       |\n                       v\n                  BACKEND API\n                    /     \\\n                   /       \\\n                  v         v\n              DATABASE    MESSAGE QUEUE\n                              |\n                              v\n                       BACKGROUND WORKER\n\n                    MONITORING SYSTEM\n                           |\n                           v\n                    INTERNAL SERVICES\n```\n\nThe objective is to allow required communication while limiting unnecessary access.\n\n## 2. Practical: Inspect Your Network\n\nOn a Linux server, run:\n\n```bash\nip addr\n```\n\nThis displays network interfaces and their IP addresses.\n\nNext:\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nThis displays the routing table.\n\nExample:\n\n```text\ndefault via 10.0.2.1 dev eth0\n10.0.2.0/24 dev eth0\n```\n\nInterpretation:\n\n- `default` is the default route.\n- `via 10.0.2.1` identifies the next-hop gateway.\n- `dev eth0` identifies the network interface.\n- `10.0.2.0/24` identifies the directly connected network.\n\nIf the default route is missing or incorrect, the server may fail to reach external destinations.\n\n## 3. Practical: Create a Private DNS Record\n\nSuppose your database IP is:\n\n```text\n10.0.3.10\n```\n\nYou want the backend to connect using:\n\n```text\ndatabase.internal\n```\n\nA private DNS A record could be configured as:\n\n```text\ndatabase.internal    A    10.0.3.10\n```\n\nThe application connection string could then reference the hostname.\n\nExample:\n\n```env\nMONGODB_URI=mongodb://database.internal:27017/marketplace\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `MONGODB_URI` is the environment variable name.\n- `mongodb://` identifies the MongoDB connection scheme.\n- `database.internal` is the private DNS hostname.\n- `27017` is the database port.\n- `marketplace` is the database name.\n\nThe database still requires authentication and appropriate network permissions.\n\n### Test the DNS record\n\n```bash\ndig database.internal\n```\n\nExpected result should contain an answer similar to:\n\n```text\ndatabase.internal.  300  IN  A  10.0.3.10\n```\n\nThe exact output depends on the DNS resolver and record configuration.\n\nIf resolution fails, check:\n\n- Private DNS zone association.\n- DNS resolver configuration.\n- Network connectivity to the resolver.\n- Record spelling.\n- DNS caching.\n\n## 4. Practical: Test Private Service Connectivity\n\nFrom the backend server, run:\n\n```bash\nnc -vz database.internal 27017\n```\n\nThis checks whether the backend can establish a TCP connection to the database hostname and port.\n\nIf successful, the result indicates that DNS resolution and TCP connectivity worked sufficiently for the test.\n\nIt does not confirm that database authentication or queries will succeed.\n\nNext, test the application connection using the MongoDB driver or an approved database client.\n\n## 5. Practical: Configure Nginx as a Reverse Proxy\n\nSuppose your backend application listens on:\n\n```text\n127.0.0.1:3000\n```\n\nYou want Nginx to receive external HTTP requests and forward them to the backend.\n\nInstall Nginx on Ubuntu:\n\n```bash\nsudo apt update\nsudo apt install nginx\n```\n\nCreate a configuration file:\n\n```bash\nsudo nano /etc/nginx/sites-available/marketplace-api\n```\n\nAdd:\n\n```nginx\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name api.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\nThe server block defines the virtual server.\n\n`listen 80` makes Nginx listen on HTTP port 80.\n\n`server_name` identifies the hostname.\n\n`location /` matches requests under the root path.\n\n`proxy_pass` forwards the request to the local backend.\n\n`proxy_set_header Host $host` preserves the original hostname.\n\n`proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr` forwards the client's connection IP.\n\n### Enable the configuration\n\n```bash\nsudo ln -s /etc/nginx/sites-available/marketplace-api /etc/nginx/sites-enabled/\n```\n\nThis creates a symbolic link that enables the site configuration.\n\nTest the Nginx configuration:\n\n```bash\nsudo nginx -t\n```\n\nThis checks configuration syntax and validity.\n\nReload Nginx:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\nThis applies the updated configuration without performing a full service restart.\n\nCheck the service:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl status nginx\n```\n\n### Test the proxy\n\n```bash\ncurl -I http://api.example.com\n```\n\nThis requests response headers from the hostname.\n\nFor production, configure HTTPS and a valid certificate, and restrict direct access to the backend port so that traffic comes through the intended proxy.\n\n## 6. Practical: Create a Secure SSH Jump Connection\n\nSuppose you have:\n\n```text\nBastion: 203.0.113.20\nBackend: 10.0.2.10\n```\n\nFrom your administrator computer:\n\n```bash\nssh -J admin@203.0.113.20 admin@10.0.2.10\n```\n\nThis uses the bastion to reach the private backend.\n\nA useful SSH configuration:\n\n```sshconfig\nHost marketplace-bastion\n    HostName 203.0.113.20\n    User admin\n\nHost marketplace-backend\n    HostName 10.0.2.10\n    User admin\n    ProxyJump marketplace-bastion\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n**First host**\n\n```sshconfig\nHost marketplace-bastion\n```\n\nDefines a convenient name for the bastion connection.\n\n```sshconfig\nHostName 203.0.113.20\n```\n\nSpecifies its address.\n\n```sshconfig\nUser admin\n```\n\nSpecifies the login user.\n\n**Second host**\n\n```sshconfig\nHost marketplace-backend\n```\n\nDefines a convenient name for the private backend.\n\n```sshconfig\nHostName 10.0.2.10\n```\n\nSpecifies its private address.\n\n```sshconfig\nProxyJump marketplace-bastion\n```\n\nInstructs SSH to route the connection through the bastion.\n\nNow you can connect using:\n\n```bash\nssh marketplace-backend\n```\n\nThis assumes SSH keys, user permissions, routing, and firewall policies are configured correctly.\n\n## 7. Practical: Design a Load-Balanced Application\n\nSuppose you have two application servers:\n\n```text\nServer A: 10.0.2.10\nServer B: 10.0.2.11\n```\n\nNginx can distribute requests between them.\n\nExample configuration:\n\n```nginx\nupstream marketplace_backend {\n    server 10.0.2.10:3000;\n    server 10.0.2.11:3000;\n}\n\nserver {\n    listen 80;\n    server_name api.example.com;\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://marketplace_backend;\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Explain the upstream block\n\n```nginx\nupstream marketplace_backend {\n```\n\nDefines a group of backend servers.\n\n```nginx\nserver 10.0.2.10:3000;\n```\n\nAdds the first backend server.\n\n```nginx\nserver 10.0.2.11:3000;\n```\n\nAdds the second backend server.\n\nNginx can distribute requests between these servers using its configured upstream load-balancing behaviour.\n\nThe server block accepts requests and forwards them to the upstream group.\n\n### Validate the configuration\n\n```bash\nsudo nginx -t\n```\n\nReload:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl reload nginx\n```\n\nThis example demonstrates reverse-proxy load balancing. Production deployments should also implement meaningful health checks, TLS, appropriate timeouts, and capacity planning.\n\n## 8. Practical: Infrastructure as Code with Terraform\n\nLet's create a simple AWS VPC with public and private subnets.\n\nThis is an illustrative foundation rather than a complete production deployment.\n\n### Step 1: Define the VPC\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"marketplace\" {\n  cidr_block           = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n  enable_dns_support   = true\n  enable_dns_hostnames = true\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = \"marketplace-vpc\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"marketplace\" {\n```\n\nCreates a VPC resource managed by Terraform.\n\n```hcl\ncidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n```\n\nDefines the IPv4 address range.\n\n```hcl\nenable_dns_support = true\n```\n\nEnables DNS resolution support in the VPC.\n\n```hcl\nenable_dns_hostnames = true\n```\n\nEnables DNS hostnames for resources where supported.\n\nThe `tags` block assigns a descriptive name.\n\n### Step 2: Create a public subnet\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_subnet\" \"public\" {\n  vpc_id                  = aws_vpc.marketplace.id\n  cidr_block              = \"10.0.1.0/24\"\n  availability_zone       = var.availability_zone\n  map_public_ip_on_launch = true\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = \"marketplace-public\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nExplanation:\n\n- `vpc_id` associates the subnet with the VPC.\n- `cidr_block` defines the subnet address range.\n- `availability_zone` identifies its selected zone.\n- `map_public_ip_on_launch` requests public IPv4 assignment for supported instances launched into this subnet.\n- `tags` identifies the subnet.\n\n**Important:** A subnet does not become public merely because `map_public_ip_on_launch` is enabled. It also needs a route to an internet gateway, and security controls must permit the intended traffic.\n\n### Step 3: Create a private subnet\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_subnet\" \"private\" {\n  vpc_id            = aws_vpc.marketplace.id\n  cidr_block        = \"10.0.2.0/24\"\n  availability_zone = var.availability_zone\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = \"marketplace-private\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis creates another subnet inside the same VPC.\n\nIt uses a different CIDR range, avoiding overlap with the public subnet.\n\n### Step 4: Create an internet gateway\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_internet_gateway\" \"gateway\" {\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.marketplace.id\n\n  tags = {\n    Name = \"marketplace-igw\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis creates and attaches an internet gateway to the VPC.\n\n### Step 5: Create a public route table\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_route_table\" \"public\" {\n  vpc_id = aws_vpc.marketplace.id\n\n  route {\n    cidr_block = \"0.0.0.0/0\"\n    gateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.gateway.id\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis creates a route table.\n\nThe route:\n\n```hcl\ncidr_block = \"0.0.0.0/0\"\n```\n\nMatches all IPv4 destinations not covered by a more specific route.\n\n```hcl\ngateway_id = aws_internet_gateway.gateway.id\n```\n\nSends matching traffic to the internet gateway.\n\n### Step 6: Associate the public subnet\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_route_table_association\" \"public\" {\n  subnet_id      = aws_subnet.public.id\n  route_table_id = aws_route_table.public.id\n}\n```\n\nThis associates the public subnet with the public route table.\n\nWithout this association, the subnet may continue using the VPC's main route table.\n\n### What is missing?\n\nA complete production design also needs:\n\n- Private subnet route configuration.\n- NAT or private service access where required.\n- Security groups.\n- Network ACL review.\n- Additional subnets in other availability zones.\n- Load balancer configuration.\n- Logging and monitoring.\n- Appropriate IPv6 design if IPv6 is enabled.\n- High-availability and failure-recovery planning.\n\nThe example teaches composition, not a ready-to-deploy production environment.\n\n## 9. Practical: Network Troubleshooting Workflow\n\nWhen a service cannot communicate with another service, investigate the entire path.\n\nUse this sequence:\n\n### Step 1: Verify DNS\n\n```bash\ndig database.internal\n```\n\nConfirm the hostname resolves to the intended IP.\n\n### Step 2: Verify IP reachability\n\n```bash\nping 10.0.3.10\n```\n\nRemember that ICMP may be blocked even when the application port is accessible.\n\n### Step 3: Verify routing\n\n```bash\nip route\n```\n\nCheck whether the server has an appropriate route.\n\n### Step 4: Verify the service listener\n\nOn the destination server:\n\n```bash\nsudo ss -tulnp\n```\n\nConfirm that the application is listening on the expected port and interface.\n\n### Step 5: Verify firewall rules\n\n```bash\nsudo ufw status verbose\n```\n\nCheck cloud security groups and other network controls as well.\n\n### Step 6: Test the destination port\n\n```bash\nnc -vz 10.0.3.10 27017\n```\n\nCheck whether the TCP connection can be established.\n\n### Step 7: Inspect logs\n\n```bash\nsudo journalctl -u mongod --since \"10 minutes ago\"\n```\n\nThis displays recent logs for the MongoDB service on systems where it is managed under that unit name.\n\nThe goal is to identify the failing layer rather than changing random network settings.\n\n## 10. Practical Project: Build a Cloud Network Design\n\nCreate a proposed network for a marketplace with:\n\n- Public load balancer.\n- Two application servers.\n- MongoDB database.\n- Background worker.\n- Private DNS.\n- Monitoring server.\n- Administrative access.\n\n### Requirements\n\n| Component | Network requirement |\n|---|---|\n| Load balancer | Public HTTPS access |\n| Application servers | Private application connectivity |\n| Database | Backend-only access |\n| Worker | Access to required internal services |\n| DNS | Internal hostname resolution |\n| Monitoring | Approved access to monitored systems |\n| Administration | VPN or controlled bastion access |\n\n### Deliverables\n\n1. Network diagram.\n2. CIDR allocation plan.\n3. Subnet design.\n4. Routing table.\n5. Security group rules.\n6. Private DNS records.\n7. Load-balancing configuration.\n8. Administrative access strategy.\n9. Troubleshooting procedure.\n\nExplain why each component exists and what would happen if it failed.\n\n## Part 3 Summary\n\nYou have practised:\n\n- Linux network inspection.\n- Private DNS design.\n- TCP connectivity testing.\n- Nginx reverse proxy configuration.\n- SSH ProxyJump.\n- Nginx load balancing.\n- Terraform VPC and subnet creation.\n- Network troubleshooting.\n\n**Progress: Part 3 of 4**\n\n---\n\n# PART 4 OF 4: KAI ASSESSMENT — INTERMEDIATE NETWORKING AND COMPOSITION\n\nYou have completed the lesson.\n\nNow demonstrate your ability to combine cloud networking components into complete architectures.\n\n## SECTION A: MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS\n\n**1. What is network composition?**\n\nA. Installing a browser on a server.  \nB. Combining networking components into a working architecture.  \nC. Increasing CPU memory.  \nD. Encrypting a database.\n\n**2. What is the primary purpose of network segmentation?**\n\nA. Increase the number of public IP addresses.  \nB. Divide a network into controlled security zones.  \nC. Disable DNS.  \nD. Eliminate routing tables.\n\n**3. In a hub-and-spoke architecture, what is the hub?**\n\nA. A central network connecting other networks.  \nB. A database server.  \nC. A public website.  \nD. A DNS record.\n\n**4. What is the primary purpose of a NAT gateway in a common private-subnet architecture?**\n\nA. Provide outbound internet connectivity without assigning public IPs to private instances.  \nB. Replace authentication.  \nC. Store database records.  \nD. Automatically encrypt application code.\n\n**5. Which service translates domain names into network information?**\n\nA. DHCP  \nB. DNS  \nC. FTP  \nD. SSH\n\n**6. What is a bastion host?**\n\nA. A database backup.  \nB. A controlled entry point for administrative access to private systems.  \nC. A load balancer.  \nD. A DNS server.\n\n**7. What is the main purpose of a load balancer?**\n\nA. Distribute incoming traffic across backend servers.  \nB. Replace all application servers.  \nC. Store passwords.  \nD. Allocate database storage.\n\n**8. Which technology can connect two VPCs through private IP connectivity?**\n\nA. VPC peering.  \nB. HTML.  \nC. CSS.  \nD. Git.\n\n## SECTION B: CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\n\n**9.** Explain the difference between a public subnet and a private subnet.\n\n**10.** Describe three-tier network architecture.\n\n**11.** Explain how network segmentation reduces the impact of a compromised server.\n\n**12.** What is hub-and-spoke networking? Give two advantages and one disadvantage.\n\n**13.** Explain why VPC peering is described as non-transitive.\n\n**14.** Differentiate between remote-access VPN and site-to-site VPN.\n\n**15.** Explain the purpose of private DNS in cloud environments.\n\n**16.** Differentiate between Layer 4 and Layer 7 load balancing.\n\n**17.** Explain how a reverse proxy communicates with backend services.\n\n**18.** What is network observability, and why is it important?\n\n## SECTION C: PRACTICAL QUESTIONS\n\n**19.** Write a Linux command to display network interfaces and their IP addresses.\n\n**20.** Write a command to display the Linux routing table.\n\n**21.** Write a command to inspect DNS records for `api.example.com`.\n\n**22.** Write a command to test whether TCP port 3000 is reachable on `10.0.2.10`.\n\n**23.** Explain the purpose of the following Nginx directive:\n\n```nginx\nproxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:3000;\n```\n\n**24.** Explain the purpose of the SSH option `-J`.\n\n**25.** Explain what this Terraform configuration creates:\n\n```hcl\nresource \"aws_vpc\" \"marketplace\" {\n  cidr_block = \"10.0.0.0/16\"\n}\n```\n\n## SECTION D: NETWORK TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n**26.** Your backend cannot resolve `database.internal`. Identify at least four possible causes and explain how you would investigate them.\n\n**27.** Two VPCs have a peering connection, but their servers cannot communicate. Identify the network components you would inspect.\n\n**28.** Your application has two backend servers behind a load balancer. One server is not receiving traffic. What would you investigate?\n\n**29.** An administrator can reach the bastion host but cannot access a private application server. Identify the likely causes.\n\n**30.** Your private application server can reach internal services but cannot download software updates. Explain how NAT, routes, DNS, and firewall policies may be involved.\n\n## SECTION E: ARCHITECTURE DESIGN\n\n**31. Scenario:**\n\nA company has four cloud environments:\n\n- Development.\n- Testing.\n- Production.\n- Management.\n\nDesign a hub-and-spoke network architecture.\n\nExplain:\n\n- The role of the hub.\n- The role of each spoke.\n- Which networks should communicate.\n- How centralized egress works.\n- How administrative access is secured.\n\n**32. Scenario:**\n\nYour marketplace has:\n\n- One public load balancer.\n- Two application servers in separate availability zones.\n- One private MongoDB deployment.\n- One background worker.\n- One private DNS zone.\n\nCreate a network diagram and explain the communication path from a customer request to a database operation.\n\n**33. Security challenge:**\n\nAn engineer creates a rule that allows all development servers to access the production database.\n\nExplain why this is risky and propose a more restrictive architecture.\n\n**34. Design challenge:**\n\nYou are asked to connect a company's office network to its cloud VPC.\n\nCompare:\n\n- Site-to-site VPN.\n- Dedicated private connectivity.\n- Public internet access with application-level security.\n\nExplain when each approach may be appropriate.\n\n---\n\n## FINAL PROJECT: DESIGN A COMPOSABLE CLOUD NETWORK\n\nBuild a complete cloud networking architecture for a production marketplace.\n\n### Required components\n\n| Row | Components |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | VPC · Public subnet · Private subnet |\n| 2 | Internet gateway · NAT gateway · Route tables |\n| 3 | Load balancer · Reverse proxy · Backend API |\n| 4 | Database · Message queue · Background worker |\n| 5 | Private DNS · Monitoring · Logging |\n| 6 | VPN · Bastion or managed access |\n| 7 | Security groups · Firewall policies · Network segmentation |\n\n### Project requirements\n\nYour architecture must:\n\n1. Support public HTTPS access.\n2. Keep internal services private.\n3. Support private DNS resolution.\n4. Distribute traffic across application servers.\n5. Prevent direct public database access.\n6. Provide controlled administrative connectivity.\n7. Support monitoring and troubleshooting.\n8. Consider availability across zones.\n9. Define how private servers access required external services.\n10. Explain how the design scales as the marketplace grows.\n\n### Submission format\n\n```text\nCLOUD NETWORK COMPOSITION PROJECT\n\n1. Architecture Diagram:\n\n2. VPC and CIDR Plan:\n\n3. Subnet Design:\n\n4. Routing Configuration:\n\n5. Security Group Policies:\n\n6. DNS Configuration:\n\n7. Load Balancer Design:\n\n8. Private Connectivity:\n\n9. Administrative Access:\n\n10. Monitoring and Troubleshooting:\n\n11. Scalability and Availability:\n\n12. Security Considerations:\n```\n\n## COMPLETION CHECKLIST\n\n- [ ] Understand network composition.\n- [ ] Design public and private subnet architectures.\n- [ ] Apply three-tier network segmentation.\n- [ ] Understand hub-and-spoke architecture.\n- [ ] Design multi-zone networking.\n- [ ] Explain centralized egress.\n- [ ] Understand NAT.\n- [ ] Configure private connectivity concepts.\n- [ ] Understand VPNs and bastion hosts.\n- [ ] Explain DNS and private DNS.\n- [ ] Understand load balancing.\n- [ ] Configure a basic reverse proxy.\n- [ ] Understand service-to-service communication.\n- [ ] Explain private service connectivity.\n- [ ] Understand network observability.\n- [ ] Apply Terraform to network infrastructure.\n- [ ] Troubleshoot network communication failures.\n\n# LESSON COMPLETE\n\n**Part 4 of 4 — Lesson Complete**\n\n### Recommended next lesson\n\n`Cloud Networking Fundamentals: VPNs, Bastion Hosts and Private Connectivity`\n\nThis will explore secure remote administration, encrypted network tunnels, site-to-site connectivity, private cloud access, and hybrid network communication in greater depth.";
